Thanks, we hope you find it useful.

It is hard to word the documentation so that it makes sense on all
machine types, but anyway we would agree with you that as this is a
*test* release, it would be unwise to replace latex209 with LaTeX2e at
your site.

At Manchester (a UNIX site) I have installed it as the following shell
script (latex2e)

=======
#!/bin/ksh

export TEXINPUTS=/usr/common/lib/tex3/latex2e/inputs:$TEXINPUTS:

/usr/common/bin/virtex \&latex2e $*
=======

which means essentially that I have put all the LaTeX2e files in a
site readable directory that is not in the standard TeX search path.
Then arranged TEXINPUTS so latex2e looks there first, and then in the
`standard' places later. LaTeX209 (`latex') is just a link to virtex
in the normal way for web2c TeX, and so does not `see' any of the
LaTeX2e files.

I am sure something similar can be done on DOS, Macs, VMS,.....

pro formas for .cfg files would be a good idea (although for testing
we would rather you didnt ConFiGure things too much:-)
The exception is texsys.cgf which you may *have* to configure to get a
running system on your machine. We would be *very* interested in
getting texsys.cfg settings for other TeX implementations.

